ees or participants observing the ceremony.\n\n**Conclusion**:\n- **Number of hostesses visible**: 0\n- **Appearance and positioning**: The women are elegantly dressed attendees or honorees, positioned among other guests in the main seating or stage area. Their attire is individual and formal, not uniform, and they are not performing any visible hosting duties.\n\nThese women are likely guests of honor, Nobel laureates, or members of the royal family or their entourage, rather than hostesses.\n'], 'condensed_response': '**Condensed Scene Summary (Frames\u202f0038‑0040)**  \n\nThe three consecutive frames show a formal ceremony in a large hall. Three women are visible in the crowd:\n\n| Character | Appearance | Location in Frame | Role Indicators |\n|-----------|------------|-------------------|-----------------|\n| Woman\u202f1 | Black lace evening dress | Standing in a line near the centre of the audience, slightly to the left | No uniform cues; no interaction with guests |\n| Woman\u202f2 | Red strap‑less gown with matching shawl | Adjacent to Woman\u202f1, also in the main seating area | Distinct personal attire; not positioned at an entrance or aisle |\n| Woman\u202f3 (partially visible) | Floral formal gown | Near the other two women, within the same seating block | Same lack of uniformity; no visible guiding actions |\n\n**Key observations**\n\n- **Positioning:** All three women are clustered among other guests on the main floor/stage, not at doorways, aisles, or designated welcome stations.  \n- **Attire:** Each wears a unique formal evening dress; there is no coordinated uniform that would signal a hostess role.  \n- **Interaction:** No observable gestures, gestures, or motions directed at incoming guests; they appear to be simply attending the event.  \n\n**Conclusion**\n\n- **Hostesses present:** **0**.  \n- The women are most likely honored guests (e.g., laureates, royalty, or members of the honoree’s entourage) rather than staff tasked with greeting or g